ions through the read tool.\"\nA item E06 --rating gap --confidence high --summary \"The gateway path authorizes by name prefix, lets write operations through erp_call, caches tokens without the caller identity, and the startup integrity check only logs.\" --next \"Replace prefix match with an exact operation allowlist plus argument checks, key the token cache by verified subject and instance and policy version, and fail startup on manifest mismatch.\" --violates \"authorize each action's tool, operation, resource, tenant, and arguments\" --also-affects B05"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01WDDHHnEyJCLzvrQNucp831":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-tnbgk9wh/skill"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDDHHnEyJCLzvrQNucp831","type":"tool_result","content":"Saved ledger for E06.6; run the validator before retaining yes.\nSaved E06.6 = yes. Next: E06.1 — Every governed tool path uses an enforced boundary suited to its internal HTTP, public third-party, or local stdio deployment; agent-controlled code cannot bypass it or obtain backend credentials.\n  How to verify: 1) Inventory HTTP, dynamic, CLI/code-mode, wrapper and local-process paths. 2) Trace effective egress, backend credential custody and process/file/env/network restrictions. 3) Internal private networking/mTLS is one option; public services can use agent-side egress and broker-held credentials. 4) Reject any usable unmediated path; do not require operator control of a public provider's network. Use unknown for enforcement outside this repo.\nSaved E06.1 = unknown. Next: E06.2 — The gateway validates its own incoming token audience, issuer, expiry and scopes, and obtains separately authorized, backend-specific credentials while preserving agent and delegated-subject identity.\n  How to verify: 1) Trace client-to-gateway and gateway-to-backend credentials independently. 2) Check trusted issuer, audience, grants, expiry and supported exchange/grant. 3) Reject tokens meant for other resources or unchanged incoming-token forwarding used instead of backend authorization. 4) A scoped delegated-user token is valid when the acting agent is verified separately; a vault alone does not create expiring tokens.\nSaved E06.2 = no. Next: E06.3 — Every direct, dynamic, CLI/code-mode and wrapper call receives action/resource/tenant/argument authorization, request/response validation, rate limits and decision logging.\n  How to verify: 1) Enumerate every execution path, not only tools/call. 2) Trace the same authorization, validation, quota and logging policy in each. 3) Reject alternate paths that invoke a disabled or denied tool, authorize only a tool name, or change tenant/resource authority. Use unknown for unavailable implementation evidence.\nSaved E06.3 = no. Next: E06.4 — If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n  How to verify: 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\nSaved E06.4 = no. Next: E06.5 — Under MCP 2026-07-28, relevant header/body mismatches are rejected before routing or policy relies on them, and private caches/state/task handles are isolated by verified authorization context, tenant, resource and policy version.\n  How to verify: 1) Identify supported protocol versions. 2) For 2026-07-28, trace version/method/name/parameter header validation before affected routing/policy decisions. 3) Trace cache keys/invalidation and every task/state-handle operation, including status/results and cancellation where present. 4) Reject cross-tenant/caller reads or stale grants after revocation; permit shared cache entries only when explicitly public. 5) Do not require removed protocol sessions for 2026-07-28; test legacy session binding only when that version is enabled.\nSaved E06.5 = no. Next: E06.7 — Enabled MCP extensions have recorded versions and enforced boundaries, including Tasks ownership/cancellation and Apps sandbox/CSP/bridge/tool-call authorization where present.\n  How to verify: 1) Inventory enabled extensions and versions from client/server/host configuration. 2) Trace extension-specific authorization, task ownership/status/results/cancellation, and Apps host sandbox, CSP, bridge-message validation and UI-triggered tool policy. 3) Reject a bypass or missing required boundary. 4) Answer yes for absent extensions only with configuration evidence; answer unknown for external host behavior not visible here.\nSaved E06.7 = unknown. Next: finish item E06 with `answer.py ... item E06 --rating ...`\nAdded red flag to E06.\nSaved pre-scan H001 = confirmed.\nSaved item E06 = gap. Next: E07.1 — Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.\n  How to verify: 1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5b5a778-e697-4bf4-b8d2-f31f98ce1ebe","uuid":"61b02aa3-ac0f-4fd0-ad03-f98d158fba2c","timestamp":"2026-10-20T13:32:29.240Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Saved ledger for E06.6; run the validator before retaining yes.\nSaved E06.6 = yes. Next: E06.1 — Every governed tool path uses an enforced boundary suited to its internal HTTP, public third-party, or local stdio deployment; agent-controlled code cannot bypass it or obtain backend credentials.\n  How to verify: 1) Inventory HTTP, dynamic, CLI/code-mode, wrapper and local-process paths. 2) Trace effective egress, backend credential custody and process/file/env/network restrictions. 3) Internal private networking/mTLS is one option; public services can use agent-side egress and broker-held credentials. 4) Reject any usable unmediated path; do not require operator control of a public provider's network. Use unknown for enforcement outside this repo.\nSaved E06.1 = unknown. Next: E06.2 — The gateway validates its own incoming token audience, issuer, expiry and scopes, and obtains separately authorized, backend-specific credentials while preserving agent and delegated-subject identity.\n  How to verify: 1) Trace client-to-gateway and gateway-to-backend credentials independently. 2) Check trusted issuer, audience, grants, expiry and supported exchange/grant. 3) Reject tokens meant for other resources or unchanged incoming-token forwarding used instead of backend authorization. 4) A scoped delegated-user token is valid when the acting agent is verified separately; a vault alone does not create expiring tokens.\nSaved E06.2 = no. Next: E06.3 — Every direct, dynamic, CLI/code-mode and wrapper call receives action/resource/tenant/argument authorization, request/response validation, rate limits and decision logging.\n  How to verify: 1) Enumerate every execution path, not only tools/call. 2) Trace the same authorization, validation, quota and logging policy in each. 3) Reject alternate paths that invoke a disabled or denied tool, authorize only a tool name, or change tenant/resource authority. Use unknown for unavailable implementation evidence.\nSaved E06.3 = no. Next: E06.4 — If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.\n  How to verify: 1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open.\nSaved E06.4 = no. Next: E06.5 — Under MCP 2026-07-28, relevant header/body mismatches are rejected before routing or policy relies on them, and private caches/state/task handles are isolated by verified authorization context, tenant, resource and policy version.\n  How to verify: 1) Identify supported protocol versions. 2) For 2026-07-28, trace version/method/name/parameter header validation before affected routing/policy decisions. 3) Trace cache keys/invalidation and every task/state-handle operation, including status/results and cancellation where present. 4) Reject cross-tenant/caller reads or stale grants after revocation; permit shared cache entries only when explicitly public. 5) Do not require removed protocol sessions for 2026-07-28; test legacy session bindin